{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-27366","model_call":38,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2424"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2424"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1196858521#4_2099640310","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2424","rank":2,"snippet":"Accomplishments Overview of Minimalism Key Artists Carl Andre Dan Flavin Donald Judd Kenneth Noland Richard Serra Do Not Miss Post-Minimalism Light and Space American Art Important Art and Artists of Minimalism Die Fahne Hoch! (1959) Die (1962) Lever (1966) Useful Resources on Minimalism Books Share \" , \"segment\": \"It's important to me that I don't get my hands dirty. It's not because I'm instinctively lazy. It's a declaration: art is thought.\\\" 9 of 9 Summary of Minimalism<cover>Minimalism emerged in New York in the early 1960s among artists who were self-consciously renouncing recent art they thought had become stale and academic. </cover>A wave of new influences and rediscovered styles led younger artists to question conventional boundaries between various media. The new art favored the cool over the \"dramatic\": their sculptures were frequently fabricated from industrial materials and emphasized anonymity over the expressive excess of Abstract Expressionism. Painters and sculptors avoided overt symbolism and emotional content, but instead called attention to the materiality of the works. By the end of the 1970s, Minimalism had triumphed in America and Europe through a combination of forces including museum curators, art dealers, and ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1196858521#4_2099640310","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":38,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
